comments.body:
I am 77 years young - third time learning to walk again. I have had 4  laminectomies in my spine: C-1-2, and C- 6-7, L4-5, 6-7. I did dumbass things earlier on as a runner and falling off my bike.
I am at the gym twice a week - Thursdays and Sundays for 240 minutes each visit. The rest of the week is recovery with help from anti - inflammatory chemical friends - yes live better chemically.  The following is an abbreviated list of my gym rate work;

I max out the stationary bike resistance for 30 minutes and reduce the ride by 10 degrees slowly for another 30. I do the ab-crunch machine at 240  lbs. for 4 rounds of 30 each interval. I  noticed after my last spinal operation that my grip strength was dramatically reduced and coffee mugs would slip from my hands - so I do 40 wrist curls sitting with a barbell.  To say I am a bit sore the next day is a bit of an understatement. Not pushing back on the 'blessings' of old age vs pushing back both have their price to pay. As Dylan Thomas said, "Do not go  gentle into that good night."
